## jinja2-templates ##

ansible uses jinja2 templates to enable dynamic expressions and access to variable facts . you can use templating 
with the template module.

for example :- you can create a template for a configuration file, then deploy that configuration file to 
multiple environments and supply the correct data (IP address, hostname, version) for each environment. 
You can also use templating in playbooks directly, by templating task names and more. 
You can use all the standard filters and tests included in Jinja2

## project structure ##

ansible-project/
├── inventory
├── playbook.yml
└── templates/
    └── nginx.conf.j2

 ** create an jinja template **
 mkdir templates
touch templates/nginx.conf.j2

** Put this inside templates/nginx.conf.j2: **

server {
    listen {{ nginx_port }};
    server_name {{ server_name }};

    location / {
        root /var/www/html;
        index index.html;
    }
}

** create a ansible playbook **

- name: Configure Nginx
  hosts: all
  become: true

  vars:
    nginx_port: 8080
    server_name: example.com

  tasks:
    - name: Create nginx configuration
      ansible.builtin.template:
        src: nginx.conf.j2
        dest: /etc/nginx/conf.d/myapp.conf
        mode: '0644'

    - name: Restart nginx
      ansible.builtin.service:
        name: nginx
        state: restarted

 run this ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml


 ## how it works ##

                  Ansible
                    |
                    ↓
             Reads variables
                    |
                    ↓
          Reads nginx.conf.j2
                    |
                    ↓
        Jinja2 processes {{ variables }}
                    |
                    ↓
       Generates final configuration
                    |
                    ↓
        Copies file to remote server
                    |
                    ↓
       /etc/nginx/conf.d/myapp.conf


